The {{nihongo|'''Nanboku Line'''|南北線|Nanboku-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. It is designated as '''Line 5''' of the Okami Metro network and is distinguished by its blue color ({{color box|#4169E1|border=darkgray}} Royal Blue). The line runs north–south through the city, connecting [[Kitanada (B)|Kitanada]] in the north to [[Kume Chuo]] in the south, spanning {{cvt|26.4|km|mi|abbr=on}} and serving 24 stations. It is one of the network's principal north–south corridors and a key component of the Okami Metro system.

The Nanboku Line is one of three lines in the Okami Metro network that operates 10-car [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] trains classified as '''Type A''', the largest rolling stock type in the system, with trains measuring approximately {{cvt|200|m|ft|abbr=on}} in length and {{cvt|3.0|m|ft|abbr=on}} in width. 
The line serves 10 major interchange hubs, including connections to the {{OMSN|M|00}} [[Miyoshi Line]] at [[Otemachi (M)|Otemachi]], the {{OMSN|A|00}} [[Asahi Line]] at [[Koen-Minami (A)|Koen-Minami]], the {{OMSN|T|00}} [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] at [[Kasuganohara (T)|Kasuganohara]], the {{OMSN|H|00}} [[Hitotsubashi Line]] at [[Hino (H)|Hino]], the {{OMSN|G|00}} [[Tokushige Line]] at [[Tedori Chuo]] and [[Aoyama]], the {{OMSN|S|00}} [[Shitayanagi Line]] at [[Aoyama Yagura (S)|Aoyama Yagura]], the {{OMSN|C|00}} [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] at [[Nanboku Nagashima]], the {{OMSN|K|00}} [[Tokuyama Line]] at [[Suminoe (K)|Suminoe]], the {{OMSN|N|00}} [[Nagata Line]] and {{OMSN|D|00}} [[Tedorigawa Line]] at [[Tokushige (B)|Tokushige]], and the {{OMSN|P|00}} [[Nankō Port Town Line]] at [[Kitanada (P)|Kitanada]].

==History==

===Planning and construction===
The Nanboku Line was conceived in the early 1960s as part of the second phase of expansion of the Okami Municipal Subway, which had been operating the [[Miyoshi Line]] since 1935. The rapid growth of Okami's population during the post-war economic miracle necessitated new north–south corridors to relieve congestion on the existing tram system and the Miyoshi Line. The Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau finalized the route in 1963, selecting a corridor that would connect the port area of [[Minato]] in the north with the growing residential districts of [[Kume]] in the south, passing through the densely populated [[Chiyoda]] and [[Nagata]] wards.

Construction began in 1965, utilizing cut-and-cover methods for the shallow central sections and shield tunneling for the deeper segments beneath the historic districts. The project faced significant engineering challenges, particularly in the Nagata ward area, where the line had to navigate around existing utility infrastructure and the foundations of several historic structures. Despite these challenges, construction proceeded on schedule, aided by the economic boom that provided ample funding for public works.

===Opening and early operations===
The Nanboku Line opened on {{date|1969|df=y}}, initially operating between [[Kume Chuo]] and [[Otemachi (B)|Otemachi]] stations. The opening ceremony was attended by municipal officials and marked a significant milestone in the expansion of the Okami subway network. The line was the fifth subway line to open in Okami and was designated as Line 5.

The initial fleet consisted of the [[Okami Metro 1000 series|1000 series]] trains, which were among the first rolling stock manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] specifically for the Okami Metro. These trains were 10-car formations, reflecting the high passenger demand anticipated on the corridor. The southern extension to [[Kitanada (B)|Kitanada]] was completed in stages, with the full line reaching its current 24-station configuration by the late 1970s.

===Modernization===
In 2018, as part of the privatization of the Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau, the Nanboku Line was transferred to the newly formed [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] The 1000 series trains, which had served the line for nearly five decades, were retired and replaced by the new [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] trains, also manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. The 5000 series features improved energy efficiency, enhanced passenger information systems, and compatibility with the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system.

