==Rolling stock==

The Nanboku Line currently operates the [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] trains, introduced in 2018 following the privatization of the Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau. These trains are classified as '''Type A''', the largest rolling stock type in the Okami Metro system, consisting of 10-car formations with a total length of approximately {{cvt|200|m|ft|abbr=on}} and a car width of {{cvt|3.0|m|ft|abbr=on}}. The 5000 series was manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]] and features energy-efficient propulsion, regenerative braking, and an integrated passenger information system with real-time displays.

The line previously operated the [[Okami Metro 1000 series|1000 series]] trains from its opening in 1969 until their retirement in 2018. The 1000 series was the first rolling stock type designed and built by Okami Heavy Industries for the Okami Metro, and it served the line for nearly 50 years. The final 1000 series train was withdrawn from service in March 2018, shortly before the privatization of the transportation bureau.

==Service pattern==

Trains operate on the Nanboku Line at intervals of approximately 3 minutes during morning peak hours, 5 minutes during evening peak, and 8 minutes during off-peak daytime periods, with a late-evening headway of 10 minutes. First trains depart Kume Chuo at 04:58 and Kitanada at 05:02, while last trains arrive at their respective termini shortly after 00:30. The line's end-to-end journey time is approximately 38 minutes.


==Station ridership==

The Nanboku Line carried {{formatnum:1215148}} passengers daily in FY2025, making it the eleventh-busiest line of the Okami Metro network's thirteen lines. The line's busiest station is [[Otemachi (B)|Otemachi]], which handles 115,380 daily Nanboku Line boardings; as part of the larger Otemachi interchange complex, the station handles considerably more traffic when all lines are counted together. Other heavily used stations include [[Tokushige (B)|Tokushige]] (81,834), [[Hino (B)|Hino]] (81,480), [[Aoyama Yagura (B)|Aoyama Yagura]] (78,968), and [[Aoyama (B)|Aoyama]] (78,432).

Ridership on the line reflects its role as a cross-town trunk rather than a commuter radial: the busiest stations are concentrated in the central Chiyoda and Nagata wards, where the line interchanges with most other Metro lines, while the southern Kume-ku segment stations each handle fewer than 20,000 daily boardings. The northern segment between [[Suminoe (B)|Suminoe]] and [[Kitanada (B)|Kitanada]] carries substantial port-related commuter traffic, with Suminoe handling 71,364 daily boardings.

==Operations==

===Signalling and control===
The Nanboku Line was the first line in the Okami Metro network to be equipped with communications-based train control (CBTC), which was installed during the 2018–2020 modernization program following privatization. The system permits fully automated train operation (GoA 3) with on-board attendants, allowing tighter headways than the legacy fixed-block signalling used on older lines such as the [[Miyoshi Line]]. A fallback automatic train stop system remains in place for use during CBTC maintenance windows.

===Depot===
The line's rolling stock is maintained at the Tedorigawa Depot, shared with the [[Tedorigawa Line]], located adjacent to the mid-line junction near [[Tokushige (B)|Tokushige]]. The depot includes an automated inspection hall commissioned in 2019, where 5000 series formations undergo overnight examination on a rotating four-day cycle. Heavy overhauls are performed at the Okami Metro Central Works in the Minami-Isaniwa industrial district.

===Fares and ticketing===
Fares on the Nanboku Line follow the distance-based structure of the Okami Metro network, ranging from ¥180 for travel up to 6 km to ¥320 for the full line length, with transfer discounts applied automatically when connecting to other Metro lines within 60 minutes. The line accepts the [[Oka-Pass]] contactless fare card system as well as the older magnetic farecards, which remain valid for tourists purchasing day passes.

==Safety record==

The Nanboku Line has operated without a passenger fatality since its opening in 1969, a record attributed to its fully grade-separated alignment and platform screen doors, which were retrofitted to all 24 stations during the 2018–2020 modernization. The line's most serious incident occurred in March 1994, when a northbound train struck a maintenance vehicle inside the tunnel between [[Saki]] and [[Hamagawa]], injuring two track workers; the subsequent investigation led to the adoption of stricter overnight work protection procedures across the entire Okami Metro network.

During the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] of 2026, the Nanboku Line was among the last Metro lines to be restored to full service, as its CBTC system depends on the centralized Satori network for automatic train supervision. Trains operated at reduced 15-minute headways under manual signalling for three days while the network's analogue backup systems, mandated under the [[Digital Sovereignty Act]], were brought online.

==Future==

===Southern extension to Iwamura===
The centerpiece of the line's long-term development is a southern extension of approximately {{cvt|4.2|km|mi|abbr=on}} from [[Kume Chuo]] to [[Iwamura]], a growing district deep in the island's southern river lowlands. The extension is planned with two intermediate stations and a terminal station at Iwamura, built adjacent to the existing [[Fukukyu Fukushima Line]] station there. Preliminary engineering calls for a bored tunnel beneath the Kume district followed by an elevated segment into Iwamura. The Okami Metropolitan Government's 2025 transport white paper identified the extension as the network's priority southern project, with revenue service targeted for 2031.

===Gomyo Line takeover===
Under an agreement in principle reached between [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]], the [[Gomyo Railway]], and the Okami Metropolitan Government in 2025, Okami Metro is to assume full ownership and operation of the {{nihongo|Gomyo Line|御毛線|Gomyo-sen}}, the [[Gomyo Railway]]'s 9.1-kilometer commuter line from [[Gomyo Station|Gomyo]] to [[Iwamura Station|Iwamura]]. The aging line, opened in 1947 and never modernized beyond its original 1,500 V DC catenary, has suffered from declining ridership and deferred maintenance that its private owner can no longer finance.

The takeover, scheduled to take effect with the opening of the Nanboku Line's Iwamura extension in 2031, will see the Gomyo Line absorbed into the Nanboku Line as an integral southern section. Because the extension and the Gomyo Line will meet at Iwamura, the integrated line will run continuously from [[Kitanada (B)|Kitanada]] in the north to [[Gomyo Station|Gomyo]] in the south: [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] trains will run from central Okami through Kume Chuo and Iwamura and onward along the former Gomyo Line corridor through Nogata to Gomyo, making the Nanboku Line a single north–south spine from the northern port district to the southern river lowlands. The program includes resignalling the former Gomyo Line with the same CBTC system used on the Nanboku Line, rebuilding its stations with platform screen doors, and bringing it under the Okami Metro fare structure with full IC-card interoperability.

The arrangement mirrors the absorption of private suburban branches by the metro network elsewhere in Okaiken, and transport planners project that direct Nanboku Line service will roughly triple ridership on the former Gomyo Line corridor by 2035. For connections toward [[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chuo]], passengers will transfer at Iwamura to the [[Fukukyu Fukushima Line]]; the Nanboku Line itself already serves the Okami Chuo complex directly at [[Otemachi (B)|Otemachi]].

===Platform door completion===
All 24 stations are scheduled to receive full-height platform screen doors by 2028, replacing the half-height barriers installed during the earlier modernization program. The upgrade is part of a network-wide accessibility and safety program adopted following the Digital Sovereignty Act's analogue-backup requirements.

